<section class="phero"><div class="phero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/frankfort-capitol.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/frankfort-capitol-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/frankfort-capitol.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-articles/">Guide articles</a></li><li aria-current="page">How the Appraisal Clause Works in a Kentucky Property Policy</li></ol></nav><p class="eyebrow on-dark">Guide · 7 min read · August 18, 2026</p><h1>How the Appraisal Clause Works in a Kentucky Property Policy</h1><p class="lede">A plain-English walk through the appraisal clause in most Kentucky property policies, from the written demand to the signed award, and the limits on what appraisal decides.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><p>Almost every homeowners, farm and commercial property policy sold in Kentucky contains a short paragraph headed “Appraisal.” Many owners never notice it until a claim stalls. That paragraph gives both sides a way to settle a disagreement about the amount of a loss without a lawsuit. This article explains how the Kentucky appraisal clause typically works, what it can decide and where it stops.</p>

<h2>What the clause usually says</h2>
<p>Policy forms differ, but most appraisal clauses follow the same pattern. If you and the insurer fail to agree on the amount of loss, either side may demand appraisal in writing. Each side then selects a competent and impartial appraiser. The two appraisers select an umpire. The appraisers each state the amount of loss, and if they fail to agree, they submit their differences to the umpire. A decision agreed to by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. Each side pays its own appraiser and shares the umpire and other expenses equally.</p>
<p>That is the skeleton. The details, including how many days each step takes and whether the insurer keeps its right to deny the claim afterward, vary by form. Your policy controls, so read the clause itself, not a summary.</p>

<h2>The written demand</h2>
<p>A demand does not need special language. It should identify the policy and claim, state that the parties disagree on the amount of loss, say that you are invoking appraisal under the policy, and name your appraiser if you have one. Send it in a way you can prove and keep a copy.</p>
<p>Timing matters. Demanding appraisal before you have a documented competing estimate can leave your appraiser starting from scratch. Waiting too long can create problems with the policy’s suit limitation clause, which policies set and Kentucky law limits in how short it can be. If that deadline is anywhere near, talk to a Kentucky attorney before relying on appraisal to protect you.</p>

<h2>Party appraisers</h2>
<p>Many policies give each side about 20 days after the demand to name an appraiser. The usual requirement is that the appraiser be “competent and impartial.” In practice, competence means real estimating experience with the kind of damage at issue: a hail-damaged roof in Bowling Green, a fire-damaged restaurant in Louisville or a flooded basement in Covington each need different expertise.</p>
<p>Impartiality is where disputes arise. Your appraiser is chosen by you and will advocate for a fair number, but should disclose relationships and avoid financial arrangements your policy or the circumstances would make improper. We cover this in more depth in our article on <a href="/appraisal-articles/choosing-appraiser-and-umpire/">choosing a party appraiser and an umpire</a>.</p>

<h2>The umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on an umpire, commonly within about 15 days under many forms. If they cannot, most policies allow either party to ask a court of record in the county where the property is located to appoint one. The umpire only rules on items the appraisers cannot resolve, and in many appraisals the umpire never needs to decide anything because the appraisers settle the whole claim themselves.</p>

<h2>The award</h2>
<p>An award signed by two of the three sets the amount of loss. A well-written award breaks the loss into dwelling, other structures, contents and loss of use, and shows replacement cost and actual cash value separately. That format makes it easier for the insurer to apply deductibles, prior payments and any depreciation holdback correctly.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Who acts</th><th>Typical window in many policies</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Demand</td><td>Either party</td><td>After a disagreement on amount</td></tr>
<tr><td>Name appraiser</td><td>Each party</td><td>About 20 days after demand</td></tr>
<tr><td>Select umpire</td><td>Both appraisers</td><td>About 15 days, or court appointment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimates and negotiation</td><td>Appraisers</td><td>Weeks to months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Award</td><td>Any two of three</td><td>When agreement is reached</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What appraisal decides and what it doesn’t</h2>
<p>Appraisal decides the amount of loss. It is very good at resolving disputes over:</p>
<ul>
<li>Repair versus replacement of a roof slope, siding elevation or floor.</li>
<li>Quantities, such as squares of roofing or square feet of drywall.</li>
<li>Unit pricing, labor rates and storm-surge pricing after a regional event.</li>
<li>Overhead and profit, steep and high charges, and code-related items.</li>
<li>Contents values when the inventory is agreed but the prices are not.</li>
</ul>
<p>It generally does not decide whether a loss is covered, whether an exclusion applies, whether the insurer handled the claim in bad faith or whether interest and fees are owed. Kentucky’s Unfair Claims Settlement Practices Act governs claims-handling conduct, and questions under it belong with a Kentucky attorney or a complaint to the Kentucky Department of Insurance. Causation can be a gray area: some disputes about whether damage came from the storm or from age are treated as part of the amount of loss and some are not, depending on the policy and the facts.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Read your form</b>Some policies add conditions, such as requiring a sworn proof of loss first, or say the insurer keeps its right to deny the claim after the award. Those terms change the strategy.</div>

<h2>Is appraisal right for your claim?</h2>
<p>Appraisal tends to make sense when coverage is accepted, the gap is meaningful and the loss is well documented. It tends to be a poor fit when the insurer has denied the claim outright, when the real dispute is legal, or when the difference is too small to cover the cost of an appraiser and half an umpire. Our <a href="/kentucky-appraisal-process/">step-by-step appraisal process guide</a> walks through each stage, and our <a href="/insurance-appraisal/home-insurance-appraisal/">home insurance appraisal</a> page explains how we help residential owners.</p>
<p>If you want an honest view of whether your claim fits, <a href="/contact-us/">contact us</a>. We are independent appraisers, not a law firm or public adjuster, and we will tell you if appraisal is unlikely to help.</p><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/appraisal-articles/ice-storm-frozen-pipe-claims/"><small>Guide</small><span>Ice Storm and Frozen-Pipe Claims: What Kentucky Owners Should Document</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-articles/tornado-claim-first-30-days/"><small>Guide</small><span>After a Tornado: A Kentucky Owner’s First-30-Days Claim Checklist</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/appraisal-articles/matching-discontinued-materials/"><small>Guide</small><span>Matching and Discontinued Shingles and Siding: Why Line Items Get Disputed</span><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a></div></article>
